# What the image build must not be sent.
#
# The Dockerfile runs `npm ci` and then `COPY . .`, so without this the host's
# own `node_modules` lands on top of the one the image just installed - macOS
# binaries in a Linux container - and the host's `dist` lands where the build
# is about to write. Both are large, and a build context that carries a
# hundred thousand files it will not use is a build context whose cache
# behaves in ways nobody can follow.
#
# It behaved badly here: `docker compose build frontend` reused a cached
# `COPY . .` layer and shipped an image built from source that predated the
# feature being deployed. The running container was the image last built and
# the stack check said so, which is true and was not the question. Three
# deploys went out that way before the served bundle was searched for a string
# it should have contained.
node_modules
dist
.vite

# Never needed to build, and `.env` in particular has no business in an image.
.env
.env.*
*.log
coverage
.DS_Store
